          <p>
            <strong>The SOLE C80</strong> is the closest like-for-like rival: a curved deck with magnetic
            resistance, eight levels rather than six, and a belt two inches wider, for $2,350 less. It gives
            up 70 lb of user capacity and three inches of deck length, and it has the same one-year parts
            term. For most home buyers who want a resisted curved trainer, it is the better buy.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The AssaultRunner Pro</strong> has no resistance but costs $1,250 less, carries a
            longer moving-parts warranty and has the largest base of independent reviews of any curved
            trainer. If sled pushes are a small part of your plan, it is the safer choice. Our
            <a href="/assaultrunner-pro-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">AssaultRunner Pro review</a>
            covers it in detail.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The TrueForm Trainer</strong> matches the Blitz's 400 lb rating and has a much shallower
            curve and a longer warranty, but no resistance. It is the pick for steady runners and form work
            rather than sprinting; see our <a href="/trueform-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">TrueForm buyer's guide</a>.
          </p>
