        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Bells of Steel Blitz</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The Blitz is a specialised machine with a high price and freight delivery. These are the ways buyers end up with the wrong one.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for resistance you will not use</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The magnetic brake is the Blitz’s reason to exist. If your plan is steady runs and intervals rather than sled pushes and resisted sprints, a no-resistance curved trainer such as the AssaultRunner Pro does the same running job for about $1,250 less.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Planning delivery around the machine weight, not the box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The Blitz is 325 lb, but it arrives as a 355 lb crate measuring 73 by 33 by 23 inches. Measure doorways and stairs against the crate, check whether the carrier brings it inside, and follow Bells of Steel’s advice to install it on a ground floor.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a shallow curve for long runs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A 17-degree curve is built to accelerate. Runners who want long, steady sessions often find a steep curve busy, and reviewers say the Blitz suits sprinting better than distance. If distance running is the goal, a shallower curve such as TrueForm’s or a motorized treadmill fits better.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not reading the one-year parts warranty</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Five years on the frame sounds reassuring, but the parts that wear on a curved trainer are bearings and slats, covered for one year. Ask what parts and labour cover includes, and who pays return freight within the 30-day window, before you order.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

